  "metadata": {
    "title": "CVD Diamond as a Semiconductor Platform: First-Principles Models for NV-Centre Quantum Devices, n-Type Doping, and Thermal Boundary Resistance",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.20534974",
    "publication_date": "2026-06-04",
    "description": "<p>Abstract. Diamond synthesised by chemical vapour deposition (CVD) has re-emerged<br>as a compelling semiconductor platform owing to its extreme bandgap (5.47 eV), unrivalled intrinsic thermal conductivity (2200W m&minus;1 K&minus;1<br>for single-crystal material), critical electric field (10 MV cm&minus;1<br>), and host properties for nitrogen-vacancy (NV) spin<br>qubits. This manuscript presents complete, first-principles-grounded analytical models<br>for three application domains: (i) spin-qubit coherence and sensitivity of NV centres<br>across 4&ndash;600 K and under variable nitrogen density; (ii) thermal ionisation of donors<br>(N, P, S, Li) with full quadratic charge-neutrality solution and Brooks&ndash;Herring electron<br>mobility; and (iii) thermal boundary resistance (TBR) at diamond heterojunctions, incorporating the acoustic mismatch model (AMM), diffuse mismatch model (DMM), and<br>a calibrated hybrid framework validated against recent time-domain thermoreflectance<br>(TDTR) data for diamond&ndash;GaN, diamond&ndash;SiC, diamond&ndash;Si, and diamond&ndash;Cu interfaces.<br>All models are cross-referenced to experimental literature from 2014 to 2025 and implemented as open Python modules suitable for device simulation workflows.<br>Keywords: CVD diamond; NV centre; n-type doping; phosphorus donor; thermal<br>boundary resistance; quantum sensing; power electronics; phonon transport; GaN-ondiamond</p>",
